tilingual identity negotiation", "مشاركة أصحاب المصلحة في التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("independent audit of multilingual identity negotiation", "المراجعة المستقلة لـ التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("policy implications of multilingual identity negotiation", "الآثار على السياسات العامة لـ التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("transnational perspectives on multilingual identity negotiation", "المنظورات العابرة للحدود حول التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("transparent disclosure of multilingual identity negotiation", "الإفصاح الشفاف عن التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("systemic vulnerabilities in multilingual identity negotiation", "الهشاشة البنيوية في التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("historical contingencies behind multilingual identity negotiation", "الظروف التاريخية التي شكلت التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("jurisdictional constraints affecting multilingual identity negotiation", "قيود الاختصاص التي تؤثر على التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("critical appraisal of multilingual identity negotiation", "التقييم التحليلي لـ التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("burden of justification for multilingual identity negotiation", "عبء التبرير لـ التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("competing narratives around multilingual identity negotiation", "السرديات المتنافسة بشأن التفاوض حول الهوية متعددة اللغات")],
      grammarTitle:"Calibrated modality and epistemic stance",
      grammarArabic:"درجات الاحتمال والموقف المعرفي",
      grammarExamples:<String>["The discrepancy may have arisen during transcription.","The finding cannot by itself prove the allegation.","The witnesses must have interpreted the instruction differently."],
      listening:"In a specialist seminar on multilingual identity negotiation, Nour, the sociolinguist, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. A local school replaced bilingual materials with one supposedly neutral standard. Recorded interviews showed how language choices affected belonging across generations. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. Administrators feared division while families described the loss of cultural recognition. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Nour organised participatory workshops and revised the translation guidelines. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. The school learned that linguistic inclusion required more than adding a few translated signs. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
