gement in competency-based education reform", "مشاركة أصحاب المصلحة في إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("independent audit of competency-based education reform", "المراجعة المستقلة لـ إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("policy implications of competency-based education reform", "الآثار على السياسات العامة لـ إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("transnational perspectives on competency-based education reform", "المنظورات العابرة للحدود حول إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("transparent disclosure of competency-based education reform", "الإفصاح الشفاف عن إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("systemic vulnerabilities in competency-based education reform", "الهشاشة البنيوية في إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("historical contingencies behind competency-based education reform", "الظروف التاريخية التي شكلت إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("jurisdictional constraints affecting competency-based education reform", "قيود الاختصاص التي تؤثر على إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("critical appraisal of competency-based education reform", "التقييم التحليلي لـ إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("burden of justification for competency-based education reform", "عبء التبرير لـ إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات"),EnglishAdvancedWord("competing narratives around competency-based education reform", "السرديات المتنافسة بشأن إصلاح التعليم القائم على الكفايات")],
      grammarTitle:"Mixed conditionals and hypothetical consequences",
      grammarArabic:"الشرط المختلط والنتائج الافتراضية",
      grammarExamples:<String>["Had the records been preserved, the panel would be better informed now.","If the review were independent, more people might trust its findings.","Were the assumptions to change, the conclusion would require revision."],
      listening:"In a specialist seminar on competency-based education reform, Maha, the curriculum reform specialist, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. A proposed assessment system promised to replace memorisation with real-world projects. Pilot results showed strong engagement but inconsistent grading between schools. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. Parents feared unfair admissions outcomes while teachers welcomed greater autonomy. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Maha established common rubrics and independent moderation. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. The reform linked ambitious educational goals to practical safeguards for fairness. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
      listeningQuestion:"What principle guided Maha's response to the dispute?",
      listeningOptions:<String>["Publish the most dramatic interpretation immediately","Distinguish verified evidence from unresolved interpretations","Exclude every stakeholder who disagrees"],
      listeningAnswer:1,
      readingQuestion:"Why did Maha resist an immediate definitive answer?",
      readingOptions:<String>["The inquiry did not have any participants","The decision had already been cancelled","The records, assumptions and competing claims required scrutiny"],
      readingAnswer:2,
      writingCue:"By the time the panel published its findings, the ____ was more measured than the original announcement.",
      writingAnswer:"qualified conclusion about competency-based education reform"),
    EnglishAdvancedLesson(
      level:"C1",
      number:9,
      unitNumber:3,
      title:"Science and the Public Sphere",
      titleArabic:"العلم في المجال العام",
      storyTitle:"Science and the Public Sphere",
      storyTitleArabic:"العلم في المجال العام",
      paragraphs:<String>["At the civic debate auditorium, Dr. Amal, a science-policy liaison, encountered a controversy that had already attracted confident public interpretations. A technical report was used as a political slogan during a heated council debate. The evidentiary basis for science in public deliberation seemed persuasive at first, but the historical context of science in public deliberation complicated the accepted chronology. Rather than treating the interpretive framework for science in public deliberation as self-explanatory, Dr. Amal asked colleagues to identify the methodological assumptions behind science in public deliberation that informed the institution's language and decisions. The room fell silent when a familiar explanation suddenly appeared less secure.","Its conclusion contained qualifications omitted from campaign materials. During a painstaking examination, the institutional response to science in public deliberation became as important as the object of the dispute. A detailed examination of the underlying controversy about science in public deliberation disclosed disagreements that the initial summary had disguised. The preliminary appraisal of science in public deliberation remained provisional until the team consulted the documentary record on science in public deliberation and reconstructed the sequence of decisions. What appeared to be a minor technical discrepancy now had implications for the entire inquiry, especially for those previously excluded from it.","Researchers resisted partisan misuse while citizens demanded accessible explanations. The unresolved tension in science in public deliberation could not be settled by selecting whichever version sounded most reassuring. One participant emphasised the competing accounts of science in public deliberation, whereas another pointed to the ethical implications of science in public deliberation and demanded a more careful distinction between evidence, interpretation and preference. The discussion became uncomfortable precisely because the procedural safeguards for science in public deliberation required participants to defend the quality of their reasoning rather than the prestige of their institutions. Neither side could simply borrow authority from a title or a credential.","The following week, Dr. Amal convened an open session devoted to the public consultation on science in public deliberation and the regulatory constraints on science in public deliberation. Amal hosted a public evidence hearing with transparent questions. The critical assessment of science in public deliberation was prepared in writing, with explicit references to what had been verified and what still required testing. During the deliberative process around science in public deliberation, participants were invited to reformulate objections without caricaturing their opponents. The aim was not superficial harmony; it was a decision whose limitations could be explained to a sceptical reader without embarrassment.","By the time the panel published its findings, the qualified conclusion about science in public deliberation was more measured than the original announcement. The long-term consequences of science in public deliberation could not be reduced to a triumphant headline, because the judgement imposed practical obligations as well as interpretive restraint. The session treated scientific expertise as guidance for democratic choice rather than its substitute. In her final remarks, Dr. Amal argued that intellectual credibility depends not on eliminating every uncertainty but on showing exactly how conclusions follow from the evidence offered. The story ended with a commitment to revisit the decision if new information emerged."],
