disputes", "المعارضة القائمة على الحجة بشأن نزاعات التكافؤ في الترجمة"),EnglishAdvancedWord("hermeneutic restraint in translation equivalence disputes", "التحفظ التأويلي عند نزاعات التكافؤ في الترجمة")],
      grammarTitle:"Causality, agency and nominalised processes",
      grammarArabic:"السببية والفاعل والعمليات الاسمية",
      grammarExamples:<String>["The correlation did not warrant an attribution of cause.","The decision was framed as inevitable, obscuring who had made it.","Attribution of responsibility requires more than temporal succession."],
      listening:"In a specialist seminar on translation equivalence disputes, Nisreen, the senior literary translator, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. Two translations of a pivotal line implied incompatible motives. A dialect expression resisted any simple equivalent in the target language. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. The publisher wanted one definitive rendering while the author welcomed interpretive openness. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Nisreen annotated competing choices and consulted living speakers. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. Her final note made translation a reasoned negotiation rather than a transparent transfer. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
      listeningQuestion:"What principle guided Nisreen's response to the dispute?",
      listeningOptions:<String>["Publish the most dramatic interpretation immediately","Distinguish verified evidence from unresolved interpretations","Exclude every stakeholder who disagrees"],
      listeningAnswer:1,
      readingQuestion:"Why did Nisreen resist an immediate definitive answer?",
      readingOptions:<String>["The inquiry did not have any participants","The decision had already been cancelled","The records, assumptions and competing claims required scrutiny"],
      readingAnswer:2,
      writingCue:"By the time the panel published its findings, the ____ was more measured than the original announcement.",
      writingAnswer:"qualified conclusion about translation equivalence disputes"),
    EnglishAdvancedLesson(
      level:"C2",
      number:10,
      unitNumber:1,
      title:"The Limits of Certainty",
      titleArabic:"حدود اليقين",
      storyTitle:"The Limits of Certainty",
      storyTitleArabic:"حدود اليقين",
      paragraphs:<String>["At the research ethics colloquium, Dr. Sameer, a epistemologist, encountered a controversy that had already attracted confident public interpretations. A consensus statement converted high confidence into the language of inevitability. The evidentiary basis for epistemic certainty limits seemed persuasive at first, but the historical context of epistemic certainty limits complicated the accepted chronology. Rather than treating the interpretive framework for epistemic certainty limits as self-explanatory, Dr. Sameer asked colleagues to identify the methodological assumptions behind epistemic certainty limits that informed the institution's language and decisions. The room fell silent when a familiar explanation suddenly appeared less secure.","Its supporting studies contained unresolved measurement disagreements. During a painstaking examination, the institutional response to epistemic certainty limits became as important as the object of the dispute. A detailed examination of the underlying controversy about epistemic certainty limits disclosed disagreements that the initial summary had disguised. The preliminary appraisal of epistemic certainty limits remained provisional until the team consulted the documentary record on epistemic certainty limits and reconstructed the sequence of decisions. What appeared to be a minor technical discrepancy now had implications for the entire inquiry, especially for those previously excluded from it.","Institutional leaders feared that acknowledging doubt would appear weak. The unresolved tension in epistemic certainty limits could not be settled by selecting whichever version sounded most reassuring. One participant emphasised the competing accounts of epistemic certainty limits, whereas another pointed to the ethical implications of epistemic certainty limits and demanded a more careful distinction between evidence, interpretation and preference. The discussion became uncomfortable precisely because the procedural safeguards for epistemic certainty limits required participants to defend the quality of their reasoning rather than the prestige of their institutions. Neither side could simply borrow authority from a title or a credential.","The following week, Dr. Sameer convened an open session devoted to the public consultation on epistemic certainty limits and the regulatory constraints on epistemic certainty limits. Sameer drafted a graduated account of confidence and residual error. The critical assessment of epistemic certainty limits was prepared in writing, with explicit references to what had been verified and what still required testing. During the deliberative process around epistemic certainty limits, participants were invited to reformulate objections without caricaturing their opponents. The aim was not superficial harmony; it was a decision whose limitations could be explained to a sceptical reader without embarrassment.","By the time the panel published its findings, the qualified conclusion about epistemic certainty limits was more measured than the original announcement. The long-term consequences of epistemic certainty limits could not be reduced to a triumphant headline, because the judgement imposed practical obligations as well as interpretive restraint. The final statement demonstrated why disciplined uncertainty is not intellectual indecision. In her final remarks, Dr. Sameer argued that intellectual credibility depends not on eliminating every uncertainty but on showing exactly how conclusions follow from the evidence offered. The story ended with a commitment to revisit the decision if new information emerged.","Looking back, Dr. Sameer recognised the epistemic ambiguity in epistemic certainty limits as a central feature of the dispute, not an inconvenient flaw awaiting a clever rhetorical cure. The rhetorical subtext of epistemic certainty limits continued to shape how observers narrated the outcome: some praised the compromise, others criticised its caution, and still others questioned the categories through which the argument had been posed. None of these perspectives deserved automatic immunity from examination. A genuinely exacting account had to preserve the difference between a persuasive formulation and a proposition that could withstand sustained counterargument."],
