    EnglishAdvancedLesson(
      level:"C2",
      number:1,
      unitNumber:2,
      title:"The Language of Moral Responsibility",
      titleArabic:"لغة المسؤولية الأخلاقية",
      storyTitle:"The Language of Moral Responsibility",
      storyTitleArabic:"لغة المسؤولية الأخلاقية",
      paragraphs:<String>["At the international ethics forum, Professor Rana, a moral philosopher, encountered a controversy that had already attracted confident public interpretations. A public apology shifted blame onto vague institutional processes. The evidentiary basis for moral responsibility discourse seemed persuasive at first, but the historical context of moral responsibility discourse complicated the accepted chronology. Rather than treating the interpretive framework for moral responsibility discourse as self-explanatory, Professor Rana asked colleagues to identify the methodological assumptions behind moral responsibility discourse that informed the institution's language and decisions. The room fell silent when a familiar explanation suddenly appeared less secure.","Internal correspondence showed who had been authorised to act. During a painstaking examination, the institutional response to moral responsibility discourse became as important as the object of the dispute. A detailed examination of the underlying controversy about moral responsibility discourse disclosed disagreements that the initial summary had disguised. The preliminary appraisal of moral responsibility discourse remained provisional until the team consulted the documentary record on moral responsibility discourse and reconstructed the sequence of decisions. What appeared to be a minor technical discrepancy now had implications for the entire inquiry, especially for those previously excluded from it.","Defenders praised the apology's tone while critics examined its missing agents. The unresolved tension in moral responsibility discourse could not be settled by selecting whichever version sounded most reassuring. One participant emphasised the competing accounts of moral responsibility discourse, whereas another pointed to the ethical implications of moral responsibility discourse and demanded a more careful distinction between evidence, interpretation and preference. The discussion became uncomfortable precisely because the procedural safeguards for moral responsibility discourse required participants to defend the quality of their reasoning rather than the prestige of their institutions. Neither side could simply borrow authority from a title or a credential.","The following week, Professor Rana convened an open session devoted to the public consultation on moral responsibility discourse and the regulatory constraints on moral responsibility discourse. Rana analysed passive constructions and the distribution of responsibility. The critical assessment of moral responsibility discourse was prepared in writing, with explicit references to what had been verified and what still required testing. During the deliberative process around moral responsibility discourse, participants were invited to reformulate objections without caricaturing their opponents. The aim was not superficial harmony; it was a decision whose limitations could be explained to a sceptical reader without embarrassment.","By the time the panel published its findings, the qualified conclusion about moral responsibility discourse was more measured than the original announcement. The long-term consequences of moral responsibility discourse could not be reduced to a triumphant headline, because the judgement imposed practical obligations as well as interpretive restraint. Her critique argued that grammar can conceal agency even when every sentence is technically accurate. In her final remarks, Professor Rana argued that intellectual credibility depends not on eliminating every uncertainty but on showing exactly how conclusions follow from the evidence offered. The story ended with a commitment to revisit the decision if new information emerged.","Looking back, Professor Rana recognised the epistemic ambiguity in moral responsibility discourse as a central feature of the dispute, not an inconvenient flaw awaiting a clever rhetorical cure. The rhetorical subtext of moral responsibility discourse continued to shape how observers narrated the outcome: some praised the compromise, others criticised its caution, and still others questioned the categories through which the argument had been posed. None of these perspectives deserved automatic immunity from examination. A genuinely exacting account had to preserve the difference between a persuasive formulation and a proposition that could withstand sustained counterargument."],
