      grammarTitle:"Rhetorical concession and strategic qualification",
      grammarArabic:"التنازل البلاغي والتحفظ المقصود",
      grammarExamples:<String>["Granted, the evidence is suggestive; it is not conclusive.","Persuasive though the account sounds, its assumptions remain disputed.","Even if the claim were correct, it would not settle distributive justice reasoning."],
      listening:"In a specialist seminar on distributive justice reasoning, Professor Yasmine, the political philosopher, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. A citizens' assembly agreed on fairness but disagreed about what it required. A hypothetical case revealed incompatible assumptions about desert and need. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. Participants confused equal rules with equal consequences. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Yasmine structured the discussion around rival theories of justice. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. The assembly left with clearer disagreements and a more defensible decision procedure. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
      listeningQuestion:"What principle guided Professor Yasmine's response to the dispute?",
      listeningOptions:<String>["Publish the most dramatic interpretation immediately","Distinguish verified evidence from unresolved interpretations","Exclude every stakeholder who disagrees"],
      listeningAnswer:1,
      readingQuestion:"Why did Professor Yasmine resist an immediate definitive answer?",
      readingOptions:<String>["The inquiry did not have any participants","The decision had already been cancelled","The records, assumptions and competing claims required scrutiny"],
      readingAnswer:2,
      writingCue:"By the time the panel published its findings, the ____ was more measured than the original announcement.",
      writingAnswer:"qualified conclusion about distributive justice reasoning"),
    EnglishAdvancedLesson(
      level:"C2",
      number:4,
      unitNumber:2,
      title:"The Problem of Expert Authority",
      titleArabic:"إشكالية سلطة الخبراء",
      storyTitle:"The Problem of Expert Authority",
      storyTitleArabic:"إشكالية سلطة الخبراء",
      paragraphs:<String>["At the national advisory council, Dr. Kamal, a sociologist of expertise, encountered a controversy that had already attracted confident public interpretations. Experts issued a recommendation that affected communities had not been invited to question. The evidentiary basis for expert authority legitimacy seemed persuasive at first, but the historical context of expert authority legitimacy complicated the accepted chronology. Rather than treating the interpretive framework for expert authority legitimacy as self-explanatory, Dr. Kamal asked colleagues to identify the methodological assumptions behind expert authority legitimacy that informed the institution's language and decisions. The room fell silent when a familiar explanation suddenly appeared less secure.","A consultation log exposed the absence of local testimony. During a painstaking examination, the institutional response to expert authority legitimacy became as important as the object of the dispute. A detailed examination of the underlying controversy about expert authority legitimacy disclosed disagreements that the initial summary had disguised. The preliminary appraisal of expert authority legitimacy remained provisional until the team consulted the documentary record on expert authority legitimacy and reconstructed the sequence of decisions. What appeared to be a minor technical discrepancy now had implications for the entire inquiry, especially for those previously excluded from it.","Officials cited credentials while residents demanded accountable reasoning. The unresolved tension in expert authority legitimacy could not be settled by selecting whichever version sounded most reassuring. One participant emphasised the competing accounts of expert authority legitimacy, whereas another pointed to the ethical implications of expert authority legitimacy and demanded a more careful distinction between evidence, interpretation and preference. The discussion became uncomfortable precisely because the procedural safeguards for expert authority legitimacy required participants to defend the quality of their reasoning rather than the prestige of their institutions. Neither side could simply borrow authority from a title or a credential.","The following week, Dr. Kamal convened an open session devoted to the public consultation on expert authority legitimacy and the regulatory constraints on expert authority legitimacy. Kamal proposed a review combining technical competence with participatory scrutiny. The critical assessment of expert authority legitimacy was prepared in writing, with explicit references to what had been verified and what still required testing. During the deliberative process around expert authority legitimacy, participants were invited to reformulate objections without caricaturing their opponents. The aim was not superficial harmony; it was a decision whose limitations could be explained to a sceptical reader without embarrassment.","By the time the panel published its findings, the qualified conclusion about expert authority legitimacy was more measured than the original announcement. The long-term consequences of expert authority legitimacy could not be reduced to a triumphant headline, because the judgement imposed practical obligations as well as interpretive restraint. The revised process recognised that authority must be earned through transparency as well as expertise. In her final remarks, Dr. Kamal argued that intellectual credibility depends not on eliminating every uncertainty but on showing exactly how conclusions follow from the evidence offered. The story ended with a commitment to revisit the decision if new information emerged.","Looking back, Dr. Kamal recognised the epistemic ambiguity in expert authority legitimacy as a central feature of the dispute, not an inconvenient flaw awaiting a clever rhetorical cure. The rhetorical subtext of expert authority legitimacy continued to shape how observers narrated the outcome: some praised the compromise, others criticised its caution, and still others questioned the categories through which the argument had been posed. None of these perspectives deserved automatic immunity from examination. A genuinely exacting account had to preserve the difference between a persuasive formulation and a proposition that could withstand sustained counterargument."],
