      grammarExamples:<String>["This is not to suggest that every account is equally persuasive.","The stronger claim, however, exceeds what the evidence can sustain.","The argument is compelling only insofar as its premises are accepted."],
      listening:"In a specialist seminar on simplistic reform critique, Dr. Faisal, the public policy theorist, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. A popular proposal claimed that one reform would solve a complex housing crisis. Historical comparisons revealed contradictory effects across neighbourhoods. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. Sponsors wanted a memorable slogan while analysts argued for conditional policy design. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Faisal mapped interacting causes and documented distributional trade-offs. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. The resulting proposal was less marketable but substantially more defensible. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
