      grammarExamples:<String>["The minister's remark implied consent without explicitly granting it.","An apparently neutral term concealed an evaluative presupposition.","The ambiguity in translation theory limits was productive rather than accidental."],
      listening:"In a specialist seminar on translation theory limits, Professor Murad, the translation theorist, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. A universal translation metric rewarded literal accuracy while flattening humour. Readers' responses varied across regional and generational contexts. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. Engineers demanded comparable scores while translators insisted on situated judgment. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Murad designed a qualitative supplement that documented interpretive losses. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. The research questioned whether perfect equivalence was even a coherent ideal. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
