      grammarTitle:"Negative inversion and marked emphasis",
      grammarArabic:"القلب بعد النفي والتوكيد المتقدم",
      grammarExamples:<String>["Under no circumstances should archival silence be equated with proof.","Only rarely does a conclusion survive every counterexample.","Not until the definitions were compared did the disagreement emerge."],
      listening:"In a specialist seminar on disciplined scholarly doubt, Professor Majid, the philosopher of science, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. A scholar treated scepticism as grounds to reject every imperfect measurement. A replicated experiment showed that uncertainty could be bounded. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. Students struggled to distinguish reasonable caution from absolute disbelief. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Majid outlined thresholds for revising beliefs in light of evidence. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. The seminar presented doubt as an accountable method rather than a permanent refuge. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
