      grammarTitle:"Embedded clauses and layered attribution",
      grammarArabic:"الجمل المتداخلة ونسبة الآراء",
      grammarExamples:<String>["The claim that the record was complete, which few had questioned, proved misleading.","What the committee understood the witnesses to mean remains contested.","The objection raised by those whose evidence was excluded deserves attention."],
      listening:"In a specialist seminar on complex debate reconstruction, Dr. Hisham, the argumentation scholar, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. A decades-old debate was remembered through one widely quoted speech. The full transcript revealed objections that later summaries had omitted. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. Commentators treated the famous exchange as a decisive victory. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Hisham reconstructed competing premises and shifts in audience response. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. His analysis showed that an argument's history is rarely identical to its popular afterlife. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
