      listening:"In a specialist seminar on scholarly responsibility ethics, Professor Nasser, the academic integrity chair, explained why an early institutional statement required reconsideration. A prominent paper attracted praise despite using data shared under restrictive consent. An archived agreement clarified obligations to an affected community. A second participant pointed out that apparently accurate documents still needed contextual interpretation. The authors defended scholarly freedom while partners asked for reciprocal accountability. The discussion turned from the search for a decisive slogan to the difficult work of comparing sources, exposing assumptions, and allowing relevant objections to be heard. Nasser organised a review of permissions, benefits and harms. The audience learned that a defensible judgment need not pretend to be infallible. The board required remedies that addressed relationships as well as formal compliance. The final recommendation was therefore to distinguish verified observations from interpretive claims, document residual uncertainty, and make the reasoning accessible to those affected.",
